# The layers whose remainders must move together, worked out. One row per ordered pair of layers
# reachable from each other through parts, plus each member paired with itself.
#
# The test of a layer, worked out
#   `pm:Layer` states how a layer could be shown wrong: *if two candidate layers' remainders always
#   move together, they were never two layers.* If A is composed from B while B is composed from A,
#   no change to one leaves the other fixed, so the pair fails the definition of a layer: the two
#   are one.
#
# The same defect, seen from the sums
#   A composed layer's figure is its converted parts less what its eliminations remove, and the
#   figures of a stack are settled only if those sums have one answer. Two composed layers on a
#   loop have no single answer wherever the factors round the loop multiply to 1, which is what a
#   loop within one unit does: no evaluation order exists and the sums stop having one answer, at
#   the same moment and for the same reason. rank/evaluation_order.sqlc is the order half.
#
# One self-join, not a second walk
#   composition/descent.sqlc already holds everything each layer reaches, so matching it against
#   itself the other way round gives the pairs that reach each other. The groups fall out of a
#   window, because a layer that reaches another reaches everything that one reaches, so every
#   member sees the same set.
#
# Why the window sits outside the subquery
#   The duplicates are removed in the subquery, and the window runs over its result. A window beside
#   `SELECT DISTINCT` in one statement runs first, so `members` would count descent rows rather than
#   layers.
#
# Reading an empty result
#   Empty is the good outcome, and it is also what an unreached relation returns. It is empty on
#   this corpus.
#
# It does not replace the filer's attestation
#   `pm:Stack` requires `couplings` because nobody could check independence from the data, and it
#   still does. This relation lets the corpus contradict the filer's answer, which says more than
#   nothing and less than deciding the answer.
#
# Probe
#   Close a loop across two documents inside a transaction and roll back; both this relation and
#   checks/layers_move_together must move.
-- composition/descent.sqlc matched against itself the other way round;
-- the groups of layers that reach each other.
SELECT p.filing, p.layer, p.co_moves_with_filing, p.co_moves_with_layer,
       min(p.co_moves_with_filing || '/' || p.co_moves_with_layer) OVER w AS class,
       count(*) OVER w                                                    AS members
FROM (
    SELECT DISTINCT a.root_filing AS filing, a.root_layer AS layer,
           a.filing AS co_moves_with_filing, a.layer AS co_moves_with_layer
    FROM      (
        :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
    ) a
    JOIN      (
        :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
    ) b ON  b.root_filing = a.filing      AND b.root_layer = a.layer
        AND b.filing      = a.root_filing AND b.layer      = a.root_layer
) p
WINDOW w AS (PARTITION BY p.filing, p.layer)
